@@ -261,6 +261,47 @@ Extensions can add servers for the current session with `pi.registerMcpServer(na
 
 Changes to enabled state or exposure apply only to the current session. A file-configured server with the same name takes precedence, and `/mcp` lists the overridden registration. `pi mcp` shell commands do not load extensions and only see file-configured servers.
 
+### Host integration
+
+Extensions that show MCP servers in their own interface, for example the extension of an app that runs Pi in RPC mode, use two channels of the shared event bus, `pi.events`: `mcp:state` and `mcp:request`. Both carry plain data, so hosts declare the types below themselves.
+
+Pi publishes the state on `mcp:state` whenever it changes. Changes made in one turn of the event loop are published once. After the session shuts down, `servers` is empty.
+
+```typescript
+interface McpHostState {
+  version: 1;
+  servers: {
+    name: string;
+    // "starting" until the connection exists
+    state: "disabled" | "starting" | "connecting" | "connected" | "disconnected" | "needs-auth" | "failed" | "closed";
+    scope?: "global" | "project" | "extension";
+    source: string; // the mcp.json (or project override) or extension that defines the server
+    saves?: "global" | "project" | "session"; // where enable and disable save; absent without a scope
+    projectConfig?: string; // where enable-project and disable-project save an override, when they can
+    exposure: "codemode" | "deferred" | "direct" | "hidden";
+    toolCount?: number; // while connected
+    hasResources: boolean;
+    oauth: boolean; // signs in with OAuth: HTTP without an Authorization header or `auth`; from the config, also while disabled
+    error?: string; // last failed action, then the connection error while not connected
+  }[];
+  configErrors: string[];
+}
+```
+
+Hosts emit requests on `mcp:request`:
+
+```typescript
+pi.events.emit("mcp:request", { version: 1, action: "state", reply: (state) => render(state) });
+pi.events.emit("mcp:request", { version: 1, action: "disable", server: "docs", reply: (error) => error && show(error) });
+```
+
+- `state` replies synchronously with the current state.
+- `reconnect` and `logout` act like `/mcp reconnect` and `/mcp logout`, and wait for a server that is still starting, for example right after it was enabled. `enable` and `disable` act like the `/mcp` manager's Enable and Disable, and save where `saves` says: the `global` or `project` `mcp.json` (a project override of a global server is `project`), or the `session` alone for registered servers. `enable-project` and `disable-project` act like its Enable in this project and Disable in this project: for a global server without a project override while the project is trusted, they save an override to the project `mcp.json` that `projectConfig` names, and leave the global `mcp.json` unchanged. Other servers have no `projectConfig`, and these two reply an error for them. All four do nothing when the server already is in that state.
+- Actions reply once done, with an error message or `undefined`. `reconnect` replies the connection failure, like `/mcp reconnect`. For `enable`, connection failures show only in the state.
+- The state an action leaves is published before the action replies, so the last state a host received on `mcp:state` is current when the reply arrives.
+- Before the session starts and after it shuts down, actions reply `MCP is not running.`. An action still running when the session shuts down replies once it stopped. Requests with another `version` reply an error.
+- When another extension replaces the built-in MCP support (see below), the built-in extension still answers: `state` replies no servers, and actions reply `MCP is not running.`. When built-in MCP support is disabled with `-builtin:mcp` or `--no-mcp`, nothing replies, so hosts need a timeout.
+
 ### Replace the built-in MCP support
 
 An installed extension that registers `/mcp`, such as `pi-mcp-adapter`, replaces the built-in MCP support for sessions. Pi then does not read `mcp.json` or connect its servers in a session, and `/mcp` belongs to the extension. Remove the extension to restore the built-in behavior. To disable built-in MCP support without a replacement, disable `mcp` under Built-in in `pi config`, or set `"extensions": ["-builtin:mcp"]` in [settings](settings.md#resources). `--no-mcp` disables it for one run.
